= Epistemics =

BITwiki treats the conditions of knowing as part of the knowledge object rather than assuming every sentence has the same status.

== Why epistemics matters ==
AI systems, humans, summaries, models, and workflows can all produce confident error. A useful knowledge system needs ways to distinguish claims, guesses, interpretations, hypotheses, outputs, and verified information.

== Recovered dimensions ==
; Source
: Where did the information originate?
; Evidence
: What supports the claim?
; Provenance
: How did the material enter the system, who/what transformed it, and how did it change?
; Epistemic status
: What kind or maturity of claim is represented?
; Confidence
: How strongly does the available support justify the present representation?
; Uncertainty
: What is unknown, conditional, estimated, contested, missing, or dependent on assumptions?
; Verification
: When and how was the material checked?
; Perspective / lens
: From what conceptual, disciplinary, cultural, theoretical, or interpretive vantage is a statement being made?

== Rational objectivity ==
'''Knowledge must be grounded in evidence, structured by logic, and free from undue influence. Speculative ideas are welcome but require clear labeling.'''

